= Biological computation =

== Overview ==
'''Biological computation''' is information processing implemented through biological materials and interactions, or the engineering of biological systems to perform specified computational functions. Mechanisms include biochemical reaction networks, gene regulation, neural dynamics, molecular logic and cellular signaling. The term covers several distinct senses of computation; it must not imply that a cell is simply a miniature silicon computer.

== Physical substrate and representations ==
Where a digital circuit uses engineered electrical state transitions, a biochemical network may use molecular concentrations, binding, phosphorylation, gene expression, spatial localization and feedback. Signals can be analog, discrete, stochastic or temporally structured. Which variables count as inputs, state, memory or output must be defined within a model.

== Engineered examples ==
Gardner, Cantor and Collins (2000) built a synthetic bistable genetic toggle switch in ''Escherichia coli'', in which mutually inhibiting repressors support switchable states. Elowitz and Leibler (2000) built a synthetic oscillatory network, the ''repressilator''. These experimentally demonstrate programmable behavior in living cells; they do not show that natural biological processes are universally equivalent to general-purpose Boolean computation.

DNA-based molecular circuits exploit sequence recognition and reaction kinetics to implement conditional logic, amplification and other functions in vitro or biological contexts. Such devices carry substrate-specific trade-offs involving noise, speed, energy, biological viability and error correction.

== Bacteria as systems rather than tiny transistors ==
A bacterial cell combines metabolism, sensing, regulation, growth, repair and reproduction in an integrated physical system. Treating particular circuits as computational is useful, but ignores crucial biology if the entire organism is reduced to an abstract bit register. Agent-like responses, goals and phenotypic adaptation must be described carefully; they are not by themselves proof of reflective cognition or [[Consciousness]].

== Biology and other forms of computation ==
[[Systems biology]] investigates networks and dynamic behavior. [[Bioinformatics]] develops algorithms and data models for biological evidence. [[Genetic inheritance]] and [[Informational inheritance]] concern transmission of structure across generations and systems. These are related topics but not synonyms for biological computation.

== Validation and limitations ==
A claim that a network computes a function should specify inputs, outputs, encoding, dynamics, readout, stability and test conditions. Analogies between logic gates and gene circuits have limits: gene expression is stochastic and context-dependent, and a circuit's behavior can change across growth conditions and host strains.

